## 5. Runtime Configuration and Normal Operation

Normal configuration required to install, deploy, integrate, administer, and operate the Software is permitted and is not considered a prohibited Modification.

You may change configuration values exposed by the Software for their intended operational purpose.

Permitted configuration changes may include:

* IP addresses;
* hostnames;
* network interfaces;
* ports;
* service addresses;
* log sources;
* log destinations;
* syslog settings;
* storage locations;
* mount points;
* filesystem paths;
* retention periods;
* service names;
* program names;
* user names;
* group names;
* file ownership;
* permitted filesystem permissions;
* certificates;
* certificate paths;
* cryptographic keys;
* key paths;
* authentication settings;
* authorization settings;
* access-control settings;
* startup parameters;
* service parameters;
* environment variables;
* documented policy settings; and
* other parameters expressly intended to be configured by an administrator.

You may also perform normal administrative operations, including:

* starting services;
* stopping services;
* restarting services;
* enabling or disabling startup of the service;
* mounting supported storage;
* configuring operating-system logging;
* configuring supported syslog integration;
* configuring service accounts;
* creating backups;
* restoring authorized backups;
* installing certificates;
* replacing certificates;
* applying official updates supplied by the Licensor; and
* operating documented maintenance procedures.

The permission in this Section does not authorize changes to:

* source code;
* executable code;
* object code;
* internal program logic;
* security enforcement logic; or
* scripts containing implementation logic,

except where such changes are expressly authorized in writing by the Licensor.

